commentBody:
@David Johnson

“Across/Down” would have at least made sense in today’s puzzle.

But entering just  “hole” would have completely butchered the acrosses. Not that there is zero precedent for asymmetric rebus entries…but in this case, I just think it’s very easy to rule out in favor of the O-only solution (or, if one was really committed to using the “rebus” button, O/HOLE).

@Robco

Are you saying many constructors in the last few months were not paid?

There is some really awful professional work (reflecting pool) and some really fine amateur work (habitat for humanity).

@Robco

It’s fair that puzzles from newer constructors may be a bit more hit and miss; but I’ve seen plenty of brilliant ones from first time constructors and plenty of duds from established one. As Francis said, everyone gets paid a little; but this is no one’s full time job (except the editors) no one went to Crossword University and got a degree or gets certified  by O.R.C.A (Organization for Reviewing Constructor’s Abilities); and everyone, Zhou included, at one time started out wide-eyed and bushy-tailed and got their first puzzle published, and if they hadn’t there would be no “professional” constructors around as you put it. 
But it is nice to see a byline by someone you trust, no doubt.

@Al  in Pittsburgh 

😂. I’m not Norwegian, not a bachelor (so far, but I swear she has her moments), and am almost exactly but not quite the opposite of a farmer.

Yet I’m sure I fit the stereotype pretty well.

@Francis
So, a gimme for me. In my spare time I paint, and my current series is called “Everyday Muses”; each one shows someone engaged in some common creative endeavor, inspired by a different muse represented by a young girl. I’ve got three done so far. Here’s the one for Clio, the muse of history:

<a href="https://ibb.co/JWwD8ykj" target="_blank">https://ibb.co/JWwD8ykj</a>

@SP

It took me 30 minutes to fill about half of the puzzle. Then I missclicked something in my browser and lost all the progress I had made. I don't have it in me to give it another go. As always, I found the grid incredibly challenging but somewhat doable, and on the verge of being enjoyable. By the time I reset my progress accidentally, I had filled two themed circles but I still did not understand the theme, and I had not yet typed in the "revealers" (they were among the most puzzling entries in the whole puzzle, for me).

I think you made another typo: there's a full stop rather than a comma in the "tipping" clue.

@SP

Another very fun puzzle that I’d be happy to see as an NYT Thursday.

This one was *a lot* easier for me than “Standing Room Only”, but I really enjoyed the theme.

@Chris
I think you may be the same person who said yesterday that "Nobody knows where Bath is," and other assorted broad statements. 

I'll bet 90% of people who do these puzzles know what PATINA means, know that at least one of those two (Clio) is a MUSE, and have been to enough museums to think of ASK ME. That particular corner wasn't weak. It was tougher, and this is a Thursday puzzle. IMO, the rest of the puzzle was weak - for a Thursday.

@Bradley

It doesn't work here because the rebus squares do not contain the word HOLE (which could indeed be abbreviated to just its initial H), but instead a visual representation of a hole, formed by the letter O.

It's really not a step backwards, it's a new trick. And all you have to do to hear the happy music is enter the answers to the Across clues normally, as though there were no rebus squares at all! 😃

@Mos

The NYT has a lot of new solvers, and they’re learning the ropes. But they like to object when they haven’t mastered all of them. 

People, if an entry doesn’t work in both directions, it’s not going to be accepted!

@John

No, the rule is that the answer has to work in both directions. It’s a letter O in one direction, and it’s visually a hole in the other. 

Putting the slash answer indicates you didn’t get that the O can rpreaent a letter in one direction, and a hole in the other.

@Dan

"How do I open this bottle?"

"You unscrew it."

@Dan
It doesn't say the bottke was unscrewed. It says the bottle was opened. I often open a bottle by unscrewing the cap. I have never opened a cap by unscrewing it.
Your objection does not hold water.

@RP

Your nit is noted.  

Your nit is incorrect.

@RP
You have been incorrect many times before. The plural for 'run batted in' is 'runs batted in'. But the plural for the initialusm 'RBI' is 'RBIs'. 
It's very common usage. Sometimes it's even pronounced as an acronym, i.e. 'ribbies'.

@RP

I mean, technically you are perfectly right and you make perfect sense.

However, we could fill several steamer trunks with things about English that don't really make sense.

@sotto voce

I think I saw somewhere that when people are asked to pick a number between 1 and 20, 17 is picked more than 5% of time.

Seven and 17 have a kind of mystic to them, though 17 seems even stranger.

