@Jim in Forest Hills
1. Thursday puzzles do not always have a rebus.

2. This puzzle DOES have a rebus, as is explained in the column.

@Barry Ancona
I'm not reading others complaining it's "too easy".  I needed nine cheats, seems rather standard Thursday difficulty.

Very cool trick, though.

@Barry Ancona
After reading ALL the comments, I see it is divided, between those who didn't figure out that the "O"s stood for "hole" and those like you, whose intellects I'm in awe of.

I did figure out about the 'O's, so I'm somewhere in the middle.

@Barry Ancona
Ah but students here have lockers!
Cubbyhole is used very rarely, I can't think of when I've heard it last.

@Jane Wheelaghan

I've avoid attending baseball games because I have absolutely no desire to stand for the National Anthem.  

I'd prefer to kneel, in deep thoughts and prayers that my grandchildren's home country will not descend further into an autocracy, and become more and more like the former "communist" Soviet Union.

@Joe
hmm
Seems legit, whereas just HOLE alone dies not.

@Xword Junkie

Eh, if one slash-delimited multi-rebus is going to be accepted, I think they should both be accepted. Otherwise you’re leaning way too hard on a purely arbitrary convention (there’s no principled reason for either across or down to go before the other).

At any rate, my concession here is that a slash-delimited input format *could* have been accepted while maintaining the puzzle’s coherence, while a rebus entry of HOLE simply doesn’t work at all.

@Anonymous

But according to "convention", it seems that O/HOLE should have been accepted in each of those.

(I simply entered O in each, and never even considered using the rebus button today. But I understand the frustration of those who entered O/HOLE instead of O.)

@Andrzej
There is indeed a True Rebus.
There is not a neologismic 'rebus'.

@Andrzej

How else would you interpret the Os?

I mean, they’re not exactly *drawings*, but I did take the theme to hinge on the fact that O visually resembles a hole.

@Andrzej

Hmm, I’m not following. To me, O only makes sense as a stand-in for HOLE if you interpret it as visually depicting a hole.

Otherwise, in what sense could the letter “o” and the word “hole” be interchangeable?

@Sean McGowan

But perhaps O/HOLE should have been accepted.

Yes, that these were the only O's was noted earlier by at least one regular commenter.  ;-)

@Kevin
